00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.030 align:start position:0%
 
the demon souls remake for ps5 is an  exciting prospect for long time fans and  newcomers alike  it offers the chance to relive a  beautiful new version of from software  seminal souls game or to check out the  game that laid out the template for from  software's titles for the very first  time  but naturally there are a lot of  questions surrounding the details of the  remake effort  after all it's being handled by blue  point games alongside sony interactive  entertainment worldwide studios  blue point games is called the master of  the remaster having knocked it out of  the park with shadow of the colossus and  metal gear solid hd collection among  others  but a completely remade souls game not  involving from software  still makes fans nervous thankfully i  was able to talk to gavin moore  creative director at sie world world  studios external development and get  some answers to a few of my big  questions  having done so i feel a little more at  ease about the game  especially having learned about some of  the new features and tweaks  so settle in here are 8 things you need  to know about the demon souls remake  first up you might want to know the  history of the project and how much from  software is involved  according to moore it was blue point's  work on shadow of the colossus which he  said  redefined what a remix should be that  gave sony the confidence and knowledge  to take on demon  souls it started development immediately  after shadow of the colossus that game  was released in february 2018  but imagine production on demon souls  started much before that  on the playstation blog moore said that  the game has been in development for  three years  as for from software's involvement the  japanese developer and the game's  director hirotaka miyazaki gave their  blessing for the project to go forward  however they were not involved in the  day-to-day production which was handled  entirely by worldwide studios  and bluepoint we made sure that we  stayed true to their original vision  that's incredibly important you know  we're massive fans of miyazaki games  and so you know making sure that you  know what we created was true to his  vision wasn't you know  that was the golden standard so how much  of the game has changed  and how does blue point make sure it's  staying true to the original well the  answer to that is by both sony and blue  point being their own worst critics  according to moore  we don't put anything in this game  without you know really thinking about  how it  affects the core of the game and how it  affect that experience  and that is true to every aspect of the  game  in our interview more specifically  called out the ps5 features like haptics  visual upgrades and audio upgrades  saying that they needed to figure out  how they could use those in a way that  makes  sense in terms of combat it was  important not to change the cadence of  animations because that changes the  gameplay  and the game however moore did say that  the repulse animation shown during the  gameplay is something new that the team  has added  with proper consideration given  demon souls remake has a bunch of new  animations for different weapon types  so a spear pole arm rapier and katana  will have distinct attacks  akin to the lift and slam attack shown  in the gameplay demo  importantly however more said the  cadence is all the same as the original  so the gameplay  doesn't change the speed of the attack  is the same you're just  visually just adding you know extra  details and flourishes to really  bring that world alive one of the things  that i was really curious about was how  the ps5 solid state drive changes the  feel of the game as we saw in the  gameplay video loading and by extension  fast traveling around was  really really quick however there's a  certain kind of pacing and cadence that  is important to souls games when you die  you have the time to reflect and feel  the weight of what happened  there's so much you can feel in the time  it takes to get back into the game  so how does effectively eliminating or  at least shortening the downtime  impact the game as a whole according to  moore the team has paid attention to the  timing on certain aspects of the game  so for example traveling on an elevator  is still going to take the same amount  of time as it did in the original  this is because it's important to build  tension and think about what's ahead  that has not changed however  instantaneous loading is being used to  let the player  jump into the game quicker so loading  from the nexus into a location will be  quick because you just want to get from  an archstone to 1-1 and when you die  the game will hold a moment for a beat  to provide that time to reflect  it's just enough to take a breather  prepare and get back into it  so it seems they've paid close attention  to the pacing of downtime too  one thing that fans picked up on in the  gameplay demo was the rolls during the  vanguard battle  during this fight the player character  can be seen diving in a way that  suggested that 4-way rolling would be  back from the original when locked on  of course in that time since souls games  have become less restrictive with  evasive maneuvers available to the  player  so i asked if the remake was sticking to  the letter of the original's law or if  it was being updated  sony and blueprint previously said that  the game was going to appeal  to players that are accustomed to modern  controls and as part of that  moore confirmed that the remake will  have omni-directional evasion so you can  roll in eight directions  you know we had to make sure that we  made uh you know life improvements  to the game that people expected and  then added that making the changes  didn't  make a big difference to gameplay or the  core in any way and it was just what  people wanted  the other things that fans have been  talking about is the camera which looked  a bit different in the gameplay video it  had a bit more of a  cinematic feeling and framing more  confirmed that yes the camera has been  changed the camera from the original  demon souls as players will know had a  tendency to get stuck  on collision or would clip through the  walls which is less acceptable these  days so it had to be altered  however if you really miss it the  developers have included the option to  switch to the old camera so  in mods onwards if you don't like what  we've done we've left the old system in  there so if you want to switch to that  then please switch to  from its that  let strength be granted so the world  might be mended  if you watched the gameplay demo and  picked up on what sounded like added  dialogue from the maiden in black then  well done  gold star to you blue point and sony are  adding some new content when it comes to  writing and spoken dialogue  according to more one of the things that  the team noticed was the lack of variety  and voice over so characters would say  the same thing over and over  to remedy this the developers have added  variety what that means is that the  characters are conveying the same  information  but they're just saying it in a  different way so they feel more alive  and more natural  which is a kind of ethos that we've been  trying to do with the whole game  blueprint and sony went out and found as  many of the original cast as they could  and got them to come back and perform  their iconic roles once again  as well as voicing the new stuff all the  dialogue has been re-recorded  for the audiophiles out there it's worth  knowing that blueprint and sony have  remade thousands and thousands of sound  effects  to add into levels then using 3d audio  on ps5 brought that to life  [Applause]  more said that you'll be able to hear  arrows fly past your head and hear  creatures crawl out of slime in the  valley of deferment  sounds gross honestly  okay so yes blue point did change the  tower knight and flame lurker scene  shown in the gameplay demo  fans noted that the tower knight battle  felt a bit more bright and heroic than  in the original  while the flame locker looked to have  had its face changed entirely  after seeing the response the developers  agreed that the flame locker at least  was a step  too far and tweaked it to be closer to  the original so they are listening to  your criticisms  just remember to keep it constructive  one thing that i learned was demon souls  remake will have a number of filters  that you can play the game with  including black and white  one of the filters is a classic one so  you can use that if you'd like an  experience that harkens back to the ps3  version of the game  it's a starker and darker look that  according to moore drives a little bit  more fear into the game  more did say that he would like fans to  play through the game in the way sony  and blue point have remade at first  though just so they can see the power of  the ps5  and the treatment that they've given the  game so will demon souls have a respec  option not really but there does seem to  be a character switching option that may  allow for the use of multiple builds  more was a bit vague about it owing to  wanting to maintain some mystery around  it and also because we were rapidly  running out of time but here's what he  said  um what we have done is we've allowed  you to create you know we've updated the  character creator  when we've given thousands of thousands  of new permutations to everyone you can  create and you can play  once you've chosen a character what you  can do is  um after you've paid a number of souls  for it in the nexus you can actually  kind of store your character and  then go in and change your character in  the nexus  that's the quick interpretation i'll  leave it up to you to find out where it  is and how you do it  of course that is something completely  new to the game and we'll have to see  how it works in the game and find out  what the implications are when it's out  okay here are a few final things worth  noting about demon souls remake  kind of in a spitfire round fraction  mode has been a big mystery but no  longer  it's a fractured world that effectively  serves as a mirror mould where the map  is reversed  it sounds like it's a harder version  designed to pull the rug out from  underneath veteran players  on top of that there is no easy option  in the game there is no easy option in  demon souls it's true to the original  the reaper scythe pre-order bonus  doesn't make the game trivial as some  feared  moore also said that we have added new  content to the game hinting out more  additional weapons potentially  but everything that was in the ps3  version is in the standard edition of  the game so you won't be missing out on  the core experience  after that the old monk fight which  leverages online connectivity to turn  another player into a boss will work the  same as in the original  wall tendency is also exactly the same  the only thing that the developers have  done  is to adjust the ui to make it a little  bit easier for the player to understand  which state the world tendency is in the  healing system is also the same  so prepare to chew on some grass and  finally the all-important question  is blue point fixing the sixth arch  stone in the nexus and restoring the  content here's what moore said so  that was a long and hard discussion  um there is no sixth art stone there are  only five archetypes in the nexus it's  true to the original there's no  new arsenal there's no new world so  there you have it eight things that fans  need to know about the demon souls  remake for ps5  you can bet we'll be covering it  extensively on gamespot.com and here on  youtube.com4slashgamespot if you haven't  check out the written version of this  interview on gamespot.com  hit me up on twitter at tomorrow h and  i'll see you all in the nexus  [Music]  you

